1. LangChain, LangGraph, & LangSmith

    LangChain: A framework to "chain" different components (LLMs, databases, prompts) together. Use case: Building a basic RAG app.
    LangGraph: An extension for building stateful, multi-actor agents. It allows for "loops" (e.g., if the AI fails, it tries again). Use case: An agent that researches, writes, and then critiques its own work.
    LangSmith: A platform to debug, test, and monitor your LLM apps. Use case: Seeing exactly where a chain failed or how much a prompt cost in tokens.

2. Components of LangChain (RAG)
RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) involves:

    Document Loaders: Bringing in PDFs, URLs, or text.
    Text Splitters: Breaking docs into chunks.
    Embeddings: Turning text into numbers.
    Vector Stores: Storing those numbers.
    Retrievers: Finding the right context for a query.

3. Chunking Techniques

    Fixed-size: Chunks of exactly X characters/tokens.
    Recursive Character: Splits by paragraphs, then sentences, then words to keep context together.
    Semantic: Splits based on when the "meaning" of the text changes.

4. Vector Embedding Models

    Open Source: HuggingFace (e.g., BGE, E5 models).
    Proprietary: OpenAI (text-embedding-3-small), Cohere, or Google (Gecko).

5. Vector Databases

    Managed/Cloud: Pinecone, Weaviate, Zilliz.
    Self-hosted/Open Source: ChromaDB, FAISS, Milvus, Qdrant.

6. What is a Retriever?
A logic layer that takes a user query, searches the vector database, and pulls out the most relevant "chunks" to give to the LLM.
7. Output Template (Structured Output)
Instructions (often via Pydantic or JSON) that force the LLM to respond in a specific format instead of free-form text.
8. Query Decomposition
Breaking a complex user question ("How did sales change and who was responsible?") into smaller, searchable sub-questions.
9. Optimization for Storing Embeddings

    Indexing: Using HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World) for faster searching.
    Compression: Product Quantization (PQ) to reduce the memory size of vectors.
    Metadata Filtering: Tagging chunks (e.g., "Year: 2024") to narrow the search before it starts.

10. Offline vs. Online Pipelines

    Offline (Ingestion): The "prep" phase—loading data, chunking, embedding, and saving to the database.
    Online (Inference): The "live" phase—user asks a question, the system retrieves data and generates an answer.

11. Types of Memory

    ConversationBuffer: Stores the whole transcript.
    ConversationSummary: Summarizes the past chat to save tokens.
    Entity Memory: Remembers specific facts about a person or object.

12. Types of Tools
Functions an LLM can "call," such as: Search (Google/Tavily), Calculators, Python Interpreters, or API connectors (Slack/Jira).
13. Token Limits and Handling
Token limits are the maximum "words" an LLM can process at once.

    Handling: Chunking can be used to fit context, summarization to shorten history, or long-context models such as Gemini 1.5 Pro.

14. Types of RAG

    Naive RAG: This is a simple "Retrieve -> Generate" process.
    Advanced RAG: Adds pre-processing (query expansion) and post-processing (re-ranking results).
    Modular RAG: Allows flexible routing between different data sources.

Project Idea: "The Smart Legal/Policy Auditor"
This chatbot allows a company to upload internal policy documents (White Papers/PDFs) and ask compliance questions.
Recommended Tools and Techniques:

    LangGraph: Legal questions often need a loop; if the initial search fails, the agent should rephrase the query and retry.
    Recursive Character Chunking: This is crucial for legal text to avoid cutting sentences and losing meaning.
    Hybrid Search (Retrieval): Combine vector search (for meaning) with keyword search (for specific legal terms or section numbers).
    Grounding: Force the model to cite the exact page and paragraph used to prevent hallucination.
    Low Temperature (0.0 - 0.2): This is essential for factual accuracy over creativity




